Executive Summary
The 13 shortlisted stocks – spanning infrastructure (KEC, NBCC),
cement (ACC, Ambuja Cements), oil & gas (IOC, BPCL, GAIL, ONGC)
and banking (Kotak, Axis, SBI, HDFC, ICICI) – share several 
distinguishing traits not typical of an average Nifty50/100 
constituent. Many are either government-linked PSUs or cyclical 
commodity plays, leading to greater sensitivity to policy and
macro cycles. We find heavy government involvement (Navratna 
CPSEs, stake sales, strategic disinvestments) and sector-specific 
swings (oil price shocks, cement demand, credit cycles) drive their
returns. These names also saw major corporate actions: e.g. ACC/Ambuja’s
takeover by Adani, BPCL/IOC privatisation plans, the landmark
HDFC–HDFC Bank merger, and RBI directives (e.g. Kotak’s capital regulation).
Quantitatively, these stocks often exhibit above-median volatility and lower 
profitability metrics (thin refining/cement margins, moderate ROEs) compared 
to Nifty50 norms. We compile price/volume trends, balance-sheet ratios (debt, 
ROE, margins) and flow data to show that policy/regulatory shocks and commodity 
cycles dominate their behavior. Other Nifty companies sharing these features include
 UltraTech/HPCL (cement/oil cyclicals), Yes Bank (regulatory risk), IRCON/IRFC (PSU 
 infra financiers) – see table below. For investors, the outlook is two‑edged: potential 
 upside if cyclical trends or policy catalysts (e.g. more privatisations, infra spending) 
 materialise, versus policy/regulatory and commodity risks that have caused past sharp 
swings.

Methodology and Data Sources
We collected 5-year price, volume and fundamental data for each ticker from 
NSE/BSE filings, official results and financial databases, supplemented by
Bloomberg/Yahoo snapshots (see Appendix). Key metrics (total return, volatility,
trading volumes, debt/equity, ROE, margins) were computed and compared to median 
values for Nifty50 and Nifty100 constituents. Qualitative analysis drew on company 
reports and filings (e.g. NBCC’s corporate profile), NSE/BSE disclosures, and a broad 
set of media: primarily Reuters, Economic Times, Mint, Business Standard, BloombergQuint etc. We systematically scanned news for each company over 2019–2024, focusing on governance (promoter/ownership changes), regulatory actions, index inclusions/exclusions, FII/DII flows, corporate actions (mergers, buybacks) and recurring themes (e.g. project awards for NBCC, fuel price policies for IOC/HPCL). All findings below are rooted in these primary sources and cited accordingly.

